  "report": "Initial stage: Two focal accounts explicitly narrate Experiment acquisition, comprising five product acquisition categories: A04’s Super Saturated, Buffer Jelly, face wash and mesh moisturizer, plus A05’s Avant Guard checkout. These are account-level reports, not verified transactions or five established purchase events. No subsequent-stage evidence was supplied.\n\nA04 describes expansion across the line after favorable experiences: a sister’s introduction preceded ordering Super Saturated; wanting stronger nighttime hydration/repair prompted Buffer Jelly; favorable subsequent experience preceded buying face wash and then the newly launched mesh moisturizer. Reported benefits include lightweight, fast-drying hydration before makeup, glowy skin after Buffer Jelly, and makeup removal, hydration and no more breakouts with the wash. These are sequential, same-person reports involving multiple products, not isolated efficacy tests. Colorful packaging also receives praise, without an explicit acquisition attribution. A05 explicitly links immediate Avant Guard checkout to TikTok discovery in the context of seeking sustainability after already stopping disposable sheet masks. Neither account establishes repurchase of the same product; A04’s refill metadata cannot supply that missing narration.\n\nA01 reports putting Softwear back into an FF cart because of another commenter’s recommendation, not checkout or use. A02’s dislike of an intolerable-feeling product and consequent skipping concerns Dr. Idriss in parent context, alongside approval of its baste balm; it is not Experiment abandonment. A12’s cream 2.0 and cleanser acquisitions, post-tret moisturizer need, favorable formulation assessment and intention to try more concern Regimen Lab, not Experiment.\n\nThe two credited retailer-review origins independently repeat only the broad proposition of completed Experiment acquisition. Their specific products and reported reasons differ; no precise purchase motive is independently repeated or corroborated across source roles. A01 supplies a separate, explicitly self-attributed recommendation-to-cart consequence. No focal stock-up, gifted receipt, contemplated/completed return, or intended/completed routine substitution is narrated. A04’s claim that makeup remover is no longer needed is functional redundancy, not an explicit report of stopping it. A05’s prior sheet-mask discontinuation predates discovering Avant Guard.\n\nMaterial context, excluded from focal counts: cowrunamuck (Reddit t1_nclxocd, 2025-09-05, score 21) reports two Softwear backups because of love for the product and previously difficult website-only acquisition, plus enthusiasm for Sephora availability. They report smooth, moisturized lips despite sensitivity to other facial exfoliants, favor Softwear over Eadem, and dislike Eadem’s scent/taste while acknowledging its moisturizing effect. RowanVC (t1_ncls0z6, same thread/date, score 42) independently praises Softwear and Experiment generally; their unspecified lip-balm stash does not establish Experiment stockpiling. Their Dr. Idriss account describes stickiness, required layering and soft lips with lanolin or another mask. These are distinct contextual actors in one discussion, not focal acquisition testimony. Their Softwear praise cannot corroborate the private buying reasons of focal accounts.\n\nObserved engagement: A01 score 13, A02 score 4, A12 score 12; each is explicitly marked material_positive=false and not_interpreted. A04 and A05 each have 0 upvotes and 0 downvotes, observation time unavailable. Context scores 21 and 42 remain raw; exposure and engagement observation times are unavailable. Likes/scores establish neither additional experiences nor independence, prevalence or purchase effects.\n\nConflict posture: none observed for the bounded narrated acquisition/cart actions; broader experiences contain qualifications. A05’s strong satisfaction coexists with imperfect cheek/nose fit; A04’s thickness/oiliness concern was anticipatory and followed by praise. A02’s discomfort and A12’s small-cleanser complaint belong to other brands and coexist with positive sentiment there. Formula versions are unspecified except A12’s other-brand cream 2.0. Evidence supports individual reported actions and reasons, not market causation, representative demand or verified buying.",
